WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:05.170
Season 1 of Netflix's Stranger Things was
an instant hit, combining the mystery and

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:09.440
frights of early Stephen King with the adventurous
wonder of early Steven Spielberg.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:15.169
The powerful Eleven is clearly a nod to King's
novel Firestarter and its 1984 film adaptation,

00:00:15.169 --> 00:00:19.840
while the Spielberg references are absolutely
off-the-charts, with at least a half-dozen

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.179
visual homages to E.T. alone.

00:00:22.179 --> 00:00:28.090
The Duffer Brothers' new classic is so deeply
entrenched in 1970s and 1980s sci-fi and horror

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:32.520
nostalgia, in fact, that nearly every scene
contains a wink and a nod to one of the show's

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:33.989
many influences.

00:00:33.989 --> 00:00:37.920
But the story and characters are so engrossing,
a lot of the more obscure references can go

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.650
entirely unnoticed.

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:41.079
For example ...

00:00:41.079 --> 00:00:42.079
X-Men

00:00:42.079 --> 00:00:46.260
In Stranger Things' first episode, Dustin
challenges Will to a bike race where the loser

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:48.600
has to give up a comic book of the winner's
choosing.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.579
When Will wins, he shouts the exact issue
he wants as he rides off:

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:54.440
"Get back here!

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:57.300
I'm gonna kill you!"

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:00.740
"I'll take your X-Men 134!"

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:06.560
1980's X-Men issue 134 is the one where Jean
Gray, the X-Men's powerful psychic, becomes

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:11.270
Dark Phoenix, a cosmic being with dangerous
and unstable psychic powers.

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:15.480
This reference foreshadows the arrival of
Eleven, a girl with some mighty powerful psychic

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.549
abilities of her own.

00:01:17.549 --> 00:01:18.549
Twin Peaks

00:01:18.549 --> 00:01:22.860
In the disturbing final scene of the season,
Will excuses himself from dinner to go to

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:26.270
the bathroom, where he vomits up a creepy
otherworldly slug.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:30.899
His reality flickers away and he briefly enters
the Upside Down — the scary alternate dimension

00:01:30.899 --> 00:01:34.740
he was trapped in all season — as he stares
at himself in the mirror above the bathroom

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:35.740
sink.

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:39.210
The implication here is that the ordeal may
be over, but he's brought a little bit of

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:40.210
it back with him.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:44.880
This is similar to the final shot of David
Lynch's bizarre TV masterpiece Twin Peaks,

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:49.659
when Agent Dale Cooper smashes his head into
a bathroom mirror and cackles maniacally after

00:01:49.659 --> 00:01:54.920
escaping the alternate dimension in Twin Peaks:
the eerie "Black Lodge."

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:55.920
Alien

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:00.299
Stranger Things is filled with visual references
from the Alien franchise, particularly in

00:02:00.299 --> 00:02:02.290
the creature design of the Demogorgon.

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:06.750
The most direct reference, however, comes
in the final episode of the season, when Nancy

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:09.350
sees Jonathan being pinned to the ground by
the creature...

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:13.060
"Go to hell, you son of a bitch!"

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:16.490
It's a gender-swapped reference — with a
bit more bite — to the classic scene in

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:20.840
Aliens when Ripley, nuzzled in her power loader,
fires off a similar order at the alien Queen

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.440
as it slowly approaches Newt ...

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:25.720
"Get away from her, you bitch!"

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:31.080
The Alien references don't stop there: when
Joyce finally finds her son Will, after traveling

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:35.340
to the Upside Down, she and Hopper have to
pull a large serpent-like creature from his

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:36.340
throat.

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:40.000
This unwanted guest is likely the source of
the slug Will pukes up in the finale, making

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.050
it eerily reminiscent of the face-huggers
from the Alien franchise.

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:45.050
They Live

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:48.950
When the Duffer Brothers have Steve and Jonathan
get into a surprisingly brutal back-alley

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:53.230
brawl over the graffiti shaming of Nancy,
they're not just capturing two teenage boys

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:56.840
releasing their pent-up frustrations on each
other's faces with their fists.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:01.170
They're also paying homage to the iconic,
absurdly drawn-out back-alley battle between

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:06.790
"Rowdy" Roddy Piper and Keith David in John
Carpenter's 1989 classic They Live … except

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:11.100
in Stranger Things, it doesn't take special
glasses to see the real threat, and the cops

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.810
show up and pry Jonathan off.

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:14.860
Altered States

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:20.420
Stranger Things makes direct visual references
to 1980's Altered States with its use of sensory

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:23.960
deprivation tanks, along with other, more
subtle nods.

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:28.100
The tanks take a prominent role in the cult
science-fiction classic, as William Hurt's

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:33.310
psychologist character uses them — and powerful
drugs — to explore the limits of consciousness.

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:37.760
It's much like how Eleven uses the tanks in
Stranger Things, both willingly and against

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:42.880
her will, to amplify her psychic powers so
she can make contact with The Upside Down.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:43.980
The Goonies

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:48.060
Towards the end of the season, there's a subtle
reference to the scene from The Goonies where

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:52.590
Chunk discovers a walk-in freezer filled with
ice cream — and a dead body — in the Fratelli's

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:53.590
basement ...

00:03:53.590 --> 00:04:00.180
"They got Pralines and Cream, and they got
Mississippi Mud, and they got Chocolate Eruption!”

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:04.950
In Stranger Things, Dustin similarly discovers
a refrigerator filled with pudding cups the

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:09.480
lunch lady was hoarding — but no dead body
— in his school's cafeteria, and actor Gaten

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:12.340
Matarazzo delivers his lines a lot like Chunk
...

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:14.040
"I knew she was hoarding it.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:15.550
I knew it.

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:17.300
Always lying, saying she's out.

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:18.320
Bald-faced liar.

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:19.620
Mike!

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:21.980
I found the chocolate pudding!"

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:23.520
Thanks for watching!

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:26.000
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.410
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

